# =============================================================================
# Caddy — TLS termination, and the stack's only published port
# =============================================================================
# Caddy sits in front of `web` and terminates TLS. `web` is deliberately NOT
# published: the session cookie is Secure, so a browser keeps it only over
# HTTPS, and every request has to arrive through here rather than beside it.
#
# ## Automatic HTTPS — nothing to configure here
#
# The address comes from SITE_ADDRESS (see docker-compose.yml). Caddy picks the
# right certificate for whichever kind of name it is:
#
#   1. A public domain — `erp.example.co.za` — gets a Let's Encrypt certificate
#      and renews it, provided DNS points at this host and ports 80/443 reach it.
#   2. `localhost` (the default) is served from Caddy's own local CA, so the
#      stack works on a laptop with no DNS and no certificate authority.
#   3. A LAN-only name is also served from the local CA, but only if it is a
#      public-suffix that can never hold a real certificate — `*.internal`,
#      `*.local`, `*.home.arpa`. Use `erp.internal`, not a made-up TLD like
#      `erp.homelab`: Caddy would try to get a publicly-trusted certificate for
#      that, fail, and serve an error instead of the app.
#
# Whichever it is, http:// is redirected to https:// automatically. That is not
# cosmetic — the API sets a Secure session cookie, so a browser silently drops
# it over plain HTTP, which reads as "login does nothing".
#
# A client that has not trusted Caddy's local root CA shows a certificate
# warning (cases 2 and 3). docs/self-hosting.md shows how to fetch and trust it.
#
# ## Server-sent events
#
# The chat stream is `GET /api/chat/stream` and Caddy flushes
# `Content-Type: text/event-stream` immediately — it ignores `flush_interval`
# for exactly that type, so the stream passes through live with no tuning.
# `encode` is skipped for the same path on purpose: compressing a stream that
# exists to arrive in pieces would defeat it, and this removes any doubt.
# =============================================================================

{$SITE_ADDRESS:localhost} {
	@notstream not path /api/chat/stream
	encode @notstream zstd gzip

	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
